{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryLogBound","lean:module:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.bounded_tower_lt_target","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.bounded_tower_lt_target","kind":"theorem","line":232,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.lean#L232-L244","doc":"**The exclusion, assembled: a bounded tower stays strictly below the\ntarget, at every truncation order.** Given that the whole tail `C·h/(1-h)` falls\nshort of the gap `target - base`, no partial resummation reaches the target —\nfor every coefficient sequence within the bound, every sign pattern, and every\n`n`. This is the class-D analogue of `MultiplicityNotExtensive`'s\n`multiplicity_not_extensive`: the channel is capped by counting, not by an\nestimate of what the corpus happens to produce. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.tower_coefficient_lower_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.tower_coefficient_lower_bound","kind":"theorem","line":210,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.lean#L210-L230","doc":"**Read backwards: the coefficient a tower would need.** If the whole tower\nis to carry the quantity from `base` to `target`, its coefficient bound is at\nleast `(target - base)·(1-h)/h`. At the substrate `h = ℏ_q ≈ 0.0991`, closing\n`δ: -0.02176 → 1.8687` needs `C ≥ 17.19` at the close-packed degree and\n`C ≥ 102.1` at the chain degree.\n\n🛑 **Measured, the corpus's tower has `max_k |δ^{(k)}| = 26.14`**, so this\ndecides the chain and NOT the close-packed degree. See the header; the numbers\nhere are the *requirement*, not a demonstration that it is unmet. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.tower_geometric_abs_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.tower_geometric_abs_le","kind":"theorem","line":185,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.lean#L185-L208","doc":"**The bound that applies to a GEOMETRIC tower**, which is what the corpus's\nactually is. If the coefficients grow no faster than `r^{-k}` — i.e.\n`|a k|·r^{k+1} ≤ C`, a radius-of-convergence statement — then the tail is\nbounded by the same geometric expression in `h/r`. This is\n`tower_partial_abs_le` applied to `a k · r^{k+1}` at expansion parameter `h/r`,\nand it is the form to reach for whenever `max |a k|` is not finite or not small:\nthe corpus's measured `r ≈ 0.8729`, `h/r ≈ 0.1135`, `C ≈ 1.524` give a tail\nbound of `0.1951`, which contains the measured `|δ| = 0.02176`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.tower_partial_abs_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.tower_partial_abs_le","kind":"theorem","line":159,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.lean#L159-L183","doc":"**A tower with bounded coefficients moves its quantity by at most\n`C·h/(1-h)`, at every truncation order.** The counting is one-sided in the\ndirection that decides the question — the claim under test is that higher orders\nmake `δ` *bigger* — and the bound is uniform in `n`, so no truncation choice\nevades it and no convergence question arises. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.geom_partial_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.geom_partial_le","kind":"theorem","line":150,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.lean#L150-L157","doc":"Every partial geometric sum is below the full one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.geom_partial_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.geom_partial_eq","kind":"theorem","line":141,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.lean#L141-L148","doc":"The partial geometric sum, in the form that needs no division. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.flavour_interface_le_two_log","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.flavour_interface_le_two_log","kind":"theorem","line":122,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.lean#L122-L135","doc":"**The cap, with the flavour split as its only input.** For the corpus's\nletters the log-bound's convex weight is `w = 1 - u = 1/2 + δ_f/4`, so the whole\n`δ ≤ 2 ln q` bound reduces to `-2 ≤ δ_f ≤ 2`: one two-sided bound on one\nrendered scalar, with every other ingredient of the tower already absorbed by\n`FibreTower.interface_affine`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.interface_eq_letterTrace","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.interface_eq_letterTrace","kind":"theorem","line":110,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTowerBound.lean#L110-L120","doc":"**The affine-offset identity IS the `interface` of the log-bound file.**\nWith `b_pp = tr(σ) = z` and `b_nn = tr(σ - h) = z - h`, the interface letter\n`σ - h(1 - w)` traces to `interface b_pp b_nn w`. So the `δ ≤ 2 ln q` cap's\nconvexity hypothesis is supplied by a ring identity rather than by a numerical\ncheck at `q₀`, and is therefore untouched by any order of any fibre-level\nexpansion of `z` or of `h`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}